```

@Valid is the gateway between your controller and the validation framework. Without it, Spring deserializes the JSON request body into your DTO but **never checks the constraint annotations**. Your `@NotNull` and `@Size` annotations are just documentation — they're never enforced.

When you add `@Valid` before `@RequestBody CreateUserRequest request`, you're telling Spring: "After deserializing this JSON, run all validation constraints. If any fail, throw an exception before calling my method."

This is a crucial distinction from manual validation. With `@Valid`, your controller method has a **guarantee**: if it executes, the data is valid. You never need defensive null checks or range validations inside the method. The validation is complete before your code runs.

```java
// WITHOUT @Valid — validation annotations are ignored!
@PostMapping("/api/users")
public UserDto createUser(@RequestBody CreateUserRequest request) {
    // request.getName() could be null even with @NotNull on the field!
    // Validation annotations exist but are never checked
    return userService.create(request);
}

// WITH @Valid — validation runs before method executes
@PostMapping("/api/users")
public UserDto createUser(@Valid @RequestBody CreateUserRequest request) {
    // GUARANTEED: name is not null, email is valid, age >= 18
    // If any constraint fails, MethodArgumentNotValidException is thrown
    // and this line never executes
    return userService.create(request);
}

